N2  - Summary Modern electrowetting (EW) experiments almost exclusively use a dielectric layer to separate the conductive fluid from the electrode on the substrate. This layer makes the systems a lot more stable by preventing electrochemical reactions at the interface. This chapter derives the contact angle response in the so-called electrowetting-on-dielectric (EWOD) configuration. It considers the global response of the macroscopic contact angle, and subsequently analyzes the microscopic configuration of the drop surface in the vicinity of the contact line. Together these two approaches form the electromechanical standard model of EW. The chapter discusses the link between the standard model and the electrocapillary model. It addresses some of the limitations of the present theory and explores the differences that arise when the DC is replaced by AC voltage, as it is very commonly done in the modern EW experiments.
